### M3-02-FUP — Remove Legacy C++ Reward Logic & Enforce Python Ownership

**Description**
Clean up residual C++ reward shaping (old distance − 0.5*max_proximity) and any serialization fields no longer consumed. Codify the architectural rule: ARGoS C++ layer provides only sensor/position data & basic lifecycle; all MARL / task logic resides in Python. Update docs & code comments accordingly, and add a guard to prevent accidental reintroduction of task-specific reward code into the loop functions.

**Priority**: P0 (Post-M3-02 hygiene)
**Dependencies**: M3-02 merged
**Estimated Effort**: 1–2h

**Scope / Tasks**

* [ ] Remove obsolete reward computation from `zoo_loop_functions.cpp/.h` (distance / proximity shaping).
* [ ] Strip any unused JSON fields related to per-agent rewards from the C++ reply.
* [ ] Add a concise comment header in loop functions: "Do not implement task / reward logic here; keep simulator generic".
* [ ] Add a lightweight unit/integration check (Python) asserting C++ reply does not contain a `rewards` field (or, if present, it's empty) — fail loudly if reintroduced.
* [ ] Update `README.md` wording: replace any remaining "loop-side" phrasing with "Python-side" where it might cause confusion.
* [ ] Update this `M3-issues` document (done) referencing M3-02-FUP.

**Acceptance Criteria**

* [ ] C++ loop functions contain no task-specific reward calculations.
* [ ] Python env still produces identical metrics & rewards (no regression in existing tests).
* [ ] New guard test passes and fails if a mock `rewards` key is injected from C++.
* [ ] Docs clearly state Python is the sole location for MARL logic.

**Out of Scope**

* Adding new metrics or changing the reward formula.
* Polarization implementation (still optional / future task).

